# Findings: atomic claims with provenance. One JSON object per line.
# keys: id, claim (<=420 chars), level, scope (build|project|family), build (required for scope build), about [node ids], oracle (what was read or measured and how it was checked; required for static/verified),
#       source [pointers], unverified (what remains unchecked; always required), confirmed_by [independent pointers; required for family scope], note.
# scope: build = a fact about the named build/slice; project = one project's choice or an upstream pin, never an engine fact; family = holds across builds/games and needs confirmed_by.
{"id":"finding:oblivion-rtti-full-msvc","claim":"The Steam retail Oblivion.exe has complete MSVC RTTI: 1,715 vtables for 1,482 classes are recoverable, including the whole Ni* scene graph, the TES* forms and the Havok bhk*/hk* wrapper classes.","level":"static","scope":"build","build":"Steam retail Oblivion.exe (x86 PE32), PE timestamp 0x462392c7 (2007-04-16)","about":["game:oblivion","technique:msvc-rtti-recovery","compiler:msvc"],"oracle":"rtti_scan.py over the exe's data sections; RTTI base lists agree with known class hierarchies; runs in 0.15 s","source":["note:games/oblivion/oblivion-exe-recon-rtti-voices-gmst.md","ref:bethesda-gamebryo-re/references/engine-classes-and-exe.md"],"unverified":"Struct layouts and behaviour of the classes; other Oblivion builds (GOG, patched executables) were not scanned."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-havok-wrapper-classes","claim":"Oblivion.exe RTTI names a full Havok wrapper layer: collision objects (static, phantom, blend), shapes (MOPP tree, tri-strips, packed tri-strips, convex vertices, box, capsule, list, sphere, plane, cylinder, height field), phantoms, listeners and a constraint family (ball-and-socket, hinge, limited hinge, prismatic, ragdoll, stiff spring, malleable, fixed, generic, wheel).","level":"static","scope":"build","build":"Steam retail Oblivion.exe (x86 PE32), PE timestamp 0x462392c7","about":["game:oblivion","tech:havok","subsystem:collision","subsystem:ragdoll-constraints"],"oracle":"class names read from the RTTI dump with rtti_scan.py (85 bhk*, 51 hk*, 5 Havok* classes); names only, no behaviour read","source":["ref:bethesda-gamebryo-re/references/havok-gamebryo-collision.md","ref:bethesda-gamebryo-re/references/engine-classes-and-exe.md"],"unverified":"Which classes the shipped data actually uses; a class existing in the exe does not mean any NIF instantiates it."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-character-proxy-classes","claim":"Oblivion.exe RTTI contains a Havok character-proxy controller family: bhkCharacterController, bhkCharacterProxy, bhkCharControllerShape, bhkCharacterPointCollector and states OnGround, InAir, Jumping, Swimming, Flying and Projectile.","level":"static","scope":"build","build":"Steam retail Oblivion.exe (x86 PE32), PE timestamp 0x462392c7","about":["game:oblivion","struct:bhkCharacterController","subsystem:player-controller"],"oracle":"class names read from the RTTI dump; consistent with the runtime measurement that the player owns a character controller with a phantom and shape","source":["ref:bethesda-gamebryo-re/references/character-controller-and-stairs.md","repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/TES4_PLAYER_BODY.md"],"unverified":"How each state is entered and what it does; whether the player uses every state; step and slope rules inside the proxy."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-player-hull-convex-not-capsule","claim":"The live player controller shape is an 18-vertex convex hull, not a capsule: an eight-sided prism (ring radius 20.25 units) with a pointed cone at each end, 128 units tall, convex radius 0.70, identical while standing, walking, jumping and sneaking, and rotating with heading.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's retail Steam Oblivion run unchanged under Proton, offline, 2026-10-03; exe build not recorded in the source document","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller","struct:bhkCharacterController","technique:runtime-memory-sampler"],"oracle":"read-only 20 Hz sampler of the running process memory (player reference, controller, phantom, shape) across standing, walking stairs, jumping, sneaking, turning and three resting points; diagonal vertices agree with the executable's own cos 45 rounding","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/TES4_PLAYER_BODY.md"],"unverified":"Step height, slope limit, gait, acceleration, jump impulse, swimming and how race height scale changes the hull; one measurement session, one install, no second independent measurement."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-controller-floats-above-floor","claim":"On flat floor the original controller's hull floats: its lowest point sits about 4 units above the floor triangles and its reference position about 2.25 units below them, so a port that rests the hull on the floor stands about 3.25 units higher.","level":"inferred","scope":"build","build":"same session as the hull measurement; Vilverin starting landing","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller"],"oracle":"contact manifold distance 3.3-3.4 units at three resting points (measured); the offsets assume the contact is the floor's outer surface including its own convex radius; port rested at Z 513.0 versus the original's 509.7 on the same landing","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/TES4_PLAYER_BODY.md"],"unverified":"The surface assumption; the float is measured but not reproduced by the port."}
{"id":"finding:havok-unit-scale-about-7","claim":"Havok-space values in Oblivion are about 1/7 of game units: hull values read from the running exe divided by its own scale constant (0.14288) give 20.25 and 128 units, and the niftools factor of 7 for packed vertices and the box half-extents measured on door leaves agree.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"Oblivion (retail Steam data and exe as in the hull measurement; Vilverin hall gate NIF)","about":["game:oblivion","tech:havok","struct:bhkBoxShape","struct:bhkPackedNiTriStripsShape"],"oracle":"two measurements agree: the exe's constant applied to the sampled hull, and box half-extents times 7 reproducing the visible gate slabs","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/TES4_PLAYER_BODY.md","repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/VILVERIN_GATE.md"],"confirmed_by":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/HAVOK_COLLISION.md"],"unverified":"The constant is within 0.02% of 1/7 but not exactly it; the niftools factor was cited from that project's tools, not re-read; other Gamebryo games not measured.","note":"confirmed_by cites niftools MaxNifTools.ini (bhkScaleFactor 7) and PyFFI (packed vertices stored as nif/7) as quoted in HAVOK_COLLISION.md"}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-stair-collision-strips-unscaled","claim":"On the six stair meshes of the Vilverin slice the collision vertices live in the bhkNiTriStripsShape's NiTriStripsData in visible-mesh units: bounds match the visible mesh on four of six, MOPP scale is 1 and strip scale is (1,1,1,0), so the packed-vertex factor of 7 must not be applied.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's Oblivion Vilverin visual slice: six stair NIFs, version 20.0.0.4, user version 11/10, decoded with the OpenMW 0.52 NIF reader","about":["struct:bhkNiTriStripsShape","struct:bhkMoppBvTreeShape","game:oblivion","subsystem:collision"],"oracle":"collision bounds compared with visible-mesh bounds per axis on all six meshes; the x7 variant came out about seven times too large","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/HAVOK_COLLISION.md","exp:EXP-OO-004"],"unverified":"Packed strip shapes (zero blocks in this slice) do use the factor in niftools; U-turn and pit meshes differ by a few percent for other reasons; Fallout 3 and Skyrim not checked."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-stairs-static-layer","claim":"All six Vilverin stair meshes are fixed OL_STATIC (layer 1) stone bodies with mass 0, friction 0.3 and restitution 0.3; none uses the OL_STAIRS layer (19) or the stone-stairs material, so walkability on them cannot depend on a special stair layer.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's Oblivion Vilverin visual slice, six stair NIFs (arnhallstairs01, arnhallstairsentrance01, arnhalluturnstairs02, arwhallstairs01, arwhallstairsbridge01, arpitstairs02)","about":["subsystem:collision","struct:bhkRigidBody","game:oblivion"],"oracle":"block fields decoded for each stair body with the 20.0.0.4 layout; layer, material, motion and quality read directly","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/HAVOK_COLLISION.md"],"unverified":"Stairs in other tilesets and in later games may use OL_STAIRS; the player controller's own use of layer or material is not measured."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-collision-mesh-not-render-mesh","claim":"Authored collision of the six stair meshes has about half the triangles of the visible mesh and a measurably different surface (arwhallstairs01: median height difference 2.32 units over 245 paired rays, maximum 372.7; arnhallstairs01 median 0.55): collision geometry is not render geometry.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's Oblivion Vilverin slice, six stair NIFs, desktop OpenMW 0.52 loader with the authored-strip switch on and off","about":["subsystem:collision","game:oblivion","problem:tes4-collision-fidelity"],"oracle":"20 by 20 downward ray grid on each shape's own AABB, paired where the AABBs match","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/HAVOK_COLLISION.md","exp:EXP-OO-003"],"unverified":"This is a shape trace, not a character-controller recording; its effect on stair feel is unmeasured."}
{"id":"finding:mopp-bytecode-not-needed-for-static-import","claim":"A MOPP tree is an acceleration structure around its child shape: the child bhkNiTriStripsShape triangles can be loaded into a Bullet BVH without executing the MOPP bytes or shipping Havok's cooker.","level":"inferred","scope":"project","build":"OpenOblivion authored-strip loader on six stair meshes","about":["struct:bhkMoppBvTreeShape","tech:bullet","technique:authored-collision-translation"],"oracle":"the loader accepts MOPP-over-strips and builds a Bullet mesh whose AABB equals the visible mesh's on four of six stairs","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/HAVOK_COLLISION.md","exp:EXP-OO-003"],"unverified":"That MOPP-culled Havok queries equal queries over the full triangle set (a MOPP built from a subset or different welding would differ) was not tested against Havok."}
{"id":"finding:openmw-pin-ignores-bhk-collision","claim":"At the pinned OpenMW 0.52 revision and the Android 0.51 base, TES4 NIF collision is generated from the visible geometry when BSXFlags bit 2 is set; the reader parses bhkCollisionObject, bhkRigidBody, bhkMoppBvTreeShape and bhkNiTriStripsShape but the Bullet loader consumes none of them.","level":"static","scope":"project","build":"OpenMW 0.52 at 46bd4599203ee52ffc0f3e8edb3fc159a0303a49 (desktop pin) and the Android 0.51 base f4bec41444214a7903bebd178389ca22ca13f646","about":["engine:openmw","subsystem:collision","struct:bhkMoppBvTreeShape"],"oracle":"components/nifbullet/bulletnifloader.cpp read at both pinned revisions by the OpenOblivion project (its docs cite file and line)","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/HAVOK_COLLISION.md"],"unverified":"Later OpenMW revisions may differ; the loader itself was not re-read for this record."}
{"id":"finding:openmw-actor-constants-are-morrowind","claim":"OpenMW 0.52's actor is a Bullet shape from the rendered bounds with step-up 34, step-down 62 and maximum slope 46 degrees; these are Morrowind-era constants, not Oblivion records, and mwphysics contains no btKinematicCharacterController (it has its own movement solver and stepper).","level":"static","scope":"project","build":"OpenMW 0.52 at 46bd4599203ee52ffc0f3e8edb3fc159a0303a49","about":["engine:openmw","subsystem:player-controller","tech:bullet"],"oracle":"constants read in components/misc/constants.hpp and apps/openmw/mwphysics/constants.hpp; grep of apps/openmw/mwphysics finds no btKinematicCharacterController and lists movementsolver and stepper sources","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/HAVOK_COLLISION.md"],"unverified":"The actor-shape-from-bounds detail comes from the project doc, not re-read; later revisions untested."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-ground-speed-from-settings","claim":"The original computes player ground speed from game settings (walk min/max blended by the Speed attribute, an encumbrance term, no-weapon and sneak multipliers; run multiplies by a run multiplier plus an athletics term), not from animation state.","level":"static","scope":"build","build":"Steam retail Oblivion.exe as read by the OpenOblivion project, with the owner's Oblivion.esm settings","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller","problem:tes4-movement-parity"],"oracle":"the character speed routine and the settings it registers read in the executable; a private sample of the original walking down stairs peaked at 117.3 units/s against the formula's 116.6 for a sheathed walk","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/TES4_MOVEMENT.md"],"unverified":"Run speed was never sampled in the original; carried weight, skill changes and swim speed are unmodelled; one walking sample, from the same project."}
{"id":"finding:donor-gait-by-animation-state","claim":"OpenMW's borrowed movement picks run or sneak speed only while the actor's animation state is run or sneak; with no TES4 locomotion animations the player always moves at the 150 units/s walk speed, so key-binding changes cannot produce a second gait.","level":"verified","scope":"project","build":"OpenMW 0.52/0.51 pins as used by OpenOblivion preview builds 0.6 to 0.8","about":["engine:openmw","subsystem:player-controller","problem:tes4-movement-parity"],"oracle":"desktop probe: walk, run and sneak requests all measured 150 while the engine reported a run speed of 337.5; phone logs of 0.7 and 0.8 stayed at median 150 and 149.9","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/TES4_MOVEMENT.md","exp:EXP-OO-007"],"unverified":"Behaviour once TES4 locomotion animations exist."}
{"id":"finding:camera-smoothing-after-physics","claim":"A vertical eye-height filter run before physics (Lua onFrame) regressed ascent variation on the real stairs, while the same idea applied inside the camera update after physics did not: sample the post-physics tracked position.","level":"inferred","scope":"project","build":"OpenMW 0.52 pin (Lua onFrame runs before physics and the camera update); OpenOblivion desktop paired probes, 2026-10-01","about":["subsystem:camera","engine:openmw","problem:stair-camera-jolt","technique:paired-same-binary-probe"],"oracle":"paired same-binary runs, view vertical-velocity variation raw to filtered: Lua real ascent 1206.7 to 3008.3 (worse), real descent 2414.0 to 704.3; native post-physics ascent 1321.4 to 535.7, descent 2266.4 to 546.8","source":["exp:EXP-OO-001","exp:EXP-OO-002"],"unverified":"No run changed only the timing: the Lua and C++ filters are separate implementations, so phase is the best-fitting explanation, not an isolated cause. The Lua filter run post-physics, or the C++ one pre-physics, was not tried."}
{"id":"finding:hull-needs-support-point-walkability","claim":"Giving the player the measured pointed hull alone made the donor solver's stair hack fire (440-480 units/s, distance ratio 1.42 on the stair fixture); judging walkability from the surface under the hull centre instead of the contact normal restored normal climbing.","level":"verified","scope":"project","build":"OpenOblivion desktop build with the hull receipt, original 20-step stair fixture, software GL, one async physics worker","about":["subsystem:player-controller","engine:openmw","struct:bhkCharacterController"],"oracle":"same-binary fixtures: hull-only ratio 1.42; with the walkability patch view variation fell 31% (ascent), 34% (descent) and 43% (Vilverin) against the template box","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/TES4_PLAYER_BODY.md","exp:EXP-OO-005","exp:EXP-OO-006"],"unverified":"The original controller's own stepping rules are unmeasured; the rule fits the observed support-under-apex contact but is a port choice; phone feel of the hull build is pending."}
{"id":"finding:silent-model-failure-error-marker","claim":"When a model fails to load the donor engine substitutes an embedded error-marker mesh: a malformed player collision model became a 123-unit-wide body that could not pass a 111-unit doorway, which looked like a collision bug.","level":"verified","scope":"project","build":"OpenOblivion 0.18 on the OpenMW pins; OSG 3.6.5 ASCII reader","about":["problem:silent-asset-load-failure","engine:openmw","subsystem:collision"],"oracle":"the old file reproduces the parser errors and the blockage; the corrected file loads and the player crosses after the gate opens (native before/after probes; ARM64 OSG harness on an emulator)","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/PLAYER_COLLISION_MODEL.md","exp:EXP-OO-009"],"unverified":"Physical-phone passage retest is pending."}
{"id":"finding:android-arm64-gles-translation-crash","claim":"On the stock x86_64 Android emulator an arm64-only app that renders through GL4ES/GLES crashes at scene start with a null call inside the ARM translation layer's GLES proxy; host GPU versus software and newer images make no difference, and a native x86_64 build works.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"Android 14 and Android 16 (API 36) google_apis x86_64 emulator images, host GPU and software rendering","about":["problem:android-emulator-gles-crash","platform:android-x86_64-emulator","tech:opengl-es","tech:gl4es"],"oracle":"crash reproduced on both images and both renderers; the x86_64 build starts, renders and passes a six-check gate","source":["note:techniques/android-emulator-testing-of-arm64-native-ports.md","exp:EXP-OO-010"],"unverified":"Real arm64 phones do not use this layer; other emulator images and ARM-native hosts were not tested."}
{"id":"finding:gl4es-egl-dlopen-startup-deadlock","claim":"GL4ES's load-time eglGetDisplay can deadlock against the UI RenderThread's driver-init lock while the main thread holds the linker lock in dlopen; calling EGL14.eglGetDisplay on the main thread before the first System.loadLibrary removed the hang (3 of 4 launches hung before, 5 of 5 started after).","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"Android 16 x86_64 emulator, OpenOblivion x86_64 build, 2026-10-06","about":["problem:android-startup-deadlock","tech:gl4es","subsystem:android-port"],"oracle":"debuggerd backtraces of every thread showed the lock cycle; launch outcomes before and after the Java change","source":["note:techniques/android-emulator-testing-of-arm64-native-ports.md","exp:EXP-OO-011"],"unverified":"Not observed on real phones; samples of 4 and 5 launches are small."}
{"id":"finding:kf-transform-clips-decoded","claim":"Oblivion 20.0.0.4 KF sequences (NiControllerSequence with string-palette bone names, compressed cubic-spline channels, NIF WXYZ rotation layout) decode to 73 transform tracks for the idle and forward-walk clips; an independent PyFFI parse and de Boor evaluator agreed within 7.3e-7 on 1,525 samples.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's third-person idle and forward-walking clips, NIF version 20.0.0.4","about":["subsystem:animation","fmt:nif","game:oblivion"],"oracle":"independent PyFFI parse plus a separately written float64 de Boor evaluator over 23 compressed tracks, tolerance 2e-5","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/TES4_ANIMATION.md","exp:EXP-OO-012"],"unverified":"Decoder measurements only: no original-executable playback or locomotion comparison; two float tracks and nine tail targets are skipped."}
{"id":"finding:skeleton-names-differ-from-donor-expectation","claim":"Oblivion's male skeleton NIF has 370 blocks and 142 named nodes (Bip01 Pelvis, Bip01 Spine, Bip01 Spine1...) but no Groin, Chest, Neck, Head or Right Hand nodes, so donor code that attaches parts by Morrowind bone names fails with a Groin error.","level":"static","scope":"build","build":"owner's Oblivion data, meshes/characters/_male/skeleton.nif","about":["problem:skeleton-bone-name-mismatch","subsystem:animation","game:oblivion"],"oracle":"node names listed from the skeleton NIF with the pinned reader; the phone log shows the Groin lookup error","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/VILVERIN_GATE.md","exp:EXP-OO-012"],"unverified":"The beast skeleton (426 blocks, 160 names) was read the same way; the name mapping between the two schemes is not recorded here."}
{"id":"finding:touch-overlay-covered-use-button","claim":"At the Vilverin hall gate a thirteen-button touch overlay covered the right half of the screen including USE and the phone log had no activation line; the overlay was reduced to a move stick plus four buttons.","level":"inferred","scope":"project","build":"OpenOblivion phone build 0.10-touch-name, report from the owner's phone","about":["problem:touch-overlay-blocks-actions","subsystem:input-ui"],"oracle":"the absent activation log line while the center ray read the gate; the layout of the old overlay","source":["repo:madpai/OpenOblivion/docs/research/VILVERIN_GATE.md","exp:EXP-OO-013"],"unverified":"Phone retest of the reduced overlay is pending; the engine's same-cell door handler also hides the door for five seconds instead of animating it."}
{"id":"finding:compiler-tiebreak-residual","claim":"When the only residual in a matching MSVC 6 function is the operand order of equal-cost x87 operations inside inlined bodies, BW1's maintainers conclude it is a c2 tie-break that depends on earlier inliner IL, not on what the source means, and stop to ask whether to accept a fakematch.","level":"documented","scope":"project","build":"BW1 decomp AGENTS.md at revision be339126394b6fe35699f75b1d300623188b1628 (2026-10-06)","about":["project:bw1-decomp","problem:compiler-tiebreak-mismatch","compiler:msvc"],"source":["exp:EXP-BW1-001","catalog:openblack--bw1-decomp"],"unverified":"Stated by the BW1 maintainers; not reproduced here; MSVC 6 only."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-world-gravity-73-575","claim":"The original's Havok world gravity is (0, 0, -73.575) Havok units/s^2 (9.81 x 7.5), about 514.95 game units/s^2 (a Havok unit is 1/0.1428767293691635 game units, the executable's own constant). A controller gravity scale of 1.0 multiplies it.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's retail Steam Oblivion run unchanged under Proton, offline, 2026-10-06 (PE timestamp 0x462392c7); sampler read-only at 200 Hz","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller","struct:bhkCharacterController","tech:havok"],"oracle":"Read from the live Havok world object while the game ran; the same value reproduces every logged vertical-velocity step (g x dt) to four decimals.","source":["note:techniques/replaying-a-character-controller-from-its-own-timesteps.md","exp:EXP-OO-015"],"confirmed_by":[],"unverified":"Other builds; whether other objects (projectiles, ragdolls, horses) use the same value."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-controller-semi-implicit-per-frame","claim":"Each controller update subtracts g*dt from the vertical velocity and then moves by the new velocity, using that update's own frame time (no fixed sub-step), so the realised jump apex depends on frame rate: 69.0 units in the continuous limit, 66.8 at 60 Hz, 64.4 at 35 ms steps, 62.5 at 50 ms.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's retail Steam Oblivion run unchanged under Proton, offline, 2026-10-06 (PE timestamp 0x462392c7); sampler read-only at 200 Hz","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller","struct:bhkCharacterController","problem:jump-height-mismatch"],"oracle":"Replay of the law over every logged airborne update with its own logged dt: residual 0.000 in position and velocity on 75, 28 and 18 updates, including a 166 ms frame.","source":["note:techniques/replaying-a-character-controller-from-its-own-timesteps.md","exp:EXP-OO-015","exp:EXP-OO-016"],"confirmed_by":[],"unverified":"Hardware-GL frame rates (only about 28 Hz was recorded); behaviour at very large dt (clamping not seen)."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-jump-height-formula","claim":"Jump height is fJumpHeightMin + (fJumpHeightMax - fJumpHeightMin) x Acrobatics / 100; the launch speed is sqrt(2 g h) and the horizontal velocity is kept. Controller height fields read 69, 114 and 164 units at Acrobatics 5, 50 and 100 (min 64 exe default, max 164 master override).","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's retail Steam Oblivion run unchanged under Proton, offline, 2026-10-06 (PE timestamp 0x462392c7); sampler read-only at 200 Hz","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller","struct:bhkCharacterController","problem:tes4-movement-parity"],"oracle":"Formula read statically from the jump routine; value read from the live controller at three Acrobatics values; first airborne velocity equals sqrt(2 g h) - g dt to four decimals each time (replay residual about 4e-6).","source":["note:techniques/replaying-a-character-controller-from-its-own-timesteps.md","exp:EXP-OO-015"],"confirmed_by":[],"unverified":"Encumbrance, fatigue cost and perks (only the base formula was checked); creatures and NPCs."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-air-control-relaxation","claim":"While airborne the horizontal velocity relaxes toward the wanted ground velocity by v += f (wanted - v) per controller update, with f = fJumpMoveBase + fJumpMoveMult x Acrobatics / 100 (0 + 0.3 x 0.05 = 0.015 at Acrobatics 5; exe defaults 0 and 0.3). Momentum at takeoff is kept.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's retail Steam Oblivion run unchanged under Proton, offline, 2026-10-06 (PE timestamp 0x462392c7); sampler read-only at 200 Hz","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller","struct:bhkCharacterController"],"oracle":"Replay exact (0.000) on 28 airborne updates of a jump from a walk; +1.2 per update seen at run speed; at Acrobatics 100 the measured gain was 0.28 to 0.29 on stairs against 0.3 predicted.","source":["note:techniques/replaying-a-character-controller-from-its-own-timesteps.md","exp:EXP-OO-015"],"confirmed_by":[],"unverified":"Gain at other Acrobatics values on flat floor; whether the wanted velocity is contact-projected; per-update (not per-second) means the effect scales with frame rate."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-run-speed-355-6","claim":"The player's run speed is 355.6 units/s with Speed 40, Athletics 5 and the weapon sheathed: the live controller's first velocity after Shift+W equals the formula's run branch, walk x (fMoveRunMult + fMoveRunAthleticsMult x Athletics / 100).","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's retail Steam Oblivion run unchanged under Proton, offline, 2026-10-06 (PE timestamp 0x462392c7); sampler read-only at 200 Hz","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller","problem:tes4-movement-parity"],"oracle":"Controller velocity read at 200 Hz during Shift+W on the running original; matches the statically decoded formula to 0.1.","source":["note:techniques/replaying-a-character-controller-from-its-own-timesteps.md","finding:oblivion-ground-speed-from-settings","exp:EXP-OO-008"],"confirmed_by":[],"unverified":"Weapon drawn, sneak run, encumbrance and other stats; slope contacts reduce the realised horizontal speed."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-controller-state-codes","claim":"The controller's current-state field reads 0 on ground, 1 jumping (one update, no motion) and 2 in air, in Havok's character-state order; a separate request field holds 11 when nothing is requested.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's retail Steam Oblivion run unchanged under Proton, offline, 2026-10-06 (PE timestamp 0x462392c7); sampler read-only at 200 Hz","about":["game:oblivion","struct:bhkCharacterController","subsystem:player-controller"],"oracle":"Observed across standing, walking, jumping and landing at 200 Hz; the state-update functions were located through RTTI and read statically.","source":["note:techniques/replaying-a-character-controller-from-its-own-timesteps.md","finding:oblivion-character-proxy-classes"],"confirmed_by":[],"unverified":"Swimming, flying and projectile codes."}
{"id":"finding:oblivion-grounded-state-falls-under-gravity","claim":"On the ground the controller still adds g*dt to the vertical velocity each update and resets it on contact, so a stair flight is descended as a free fall per tread (about 38-unit drops over about 11 updates at 35 ms) without the state leaving the ground; after a jump the state switches to ground 15 to 17 units above the rest height and the hull then glides down at about g*dt.","level":"verified","scope":"build","build":"owner's retail Steam Oblivion run unchanged under Proton, offline, 2026-10-06 (PE timestamp 0x462392c7); sampler read-only at 200 Hz","about":["game:oblivion","subsystem:player-controller","struct:bhkCharacterController","problem:stair-camera-jolt"],"oracle":"Per-update traces of a walk down a stair flight and of three standing jumps; the phantom centre stays 71 units above the reference position, so it is the body; the ground and air states are told apart by a support flag the executable sets after each integration.","source":["note:techniques/replaying-a-character-controller-from-its-own-timesteps.md","exp:EXP-OO-015"],"confirmed_by":[],"unverified":"The support check's range and rule (not read); behaviour at 60 fps; other surfaces; the 15 to 17 unit capture may scale with the step."}
